<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cosimo II de' Medici</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1590">1590</a>–<a href="/years/1621">1621</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-medici">House of Medici</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cosimo II de' Medici was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a> from 1609 to 1621. He supported trade at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a> and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> fought Ottoman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> corsairs, but he is best remembered as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a>'s patron: Galileo named the moons of Jupiter the 'Medicean stars' in his honour in 1610, and Cosimo made him court mathematician and philosopher. Ill health cut short his reign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cosimo-ii-de-medici.jpg" alt="Cosimo II de' Medici" width="320" height="518" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Workshop of Justus Sustermans · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Justus_Sustermans_010.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cosimo was born on 12 May 1590, the elder son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinando-i-de-medici">Ferdinando I de' Medici</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christina-of-lorraine">Christina of Lorraine</a>. His father took care to give him a modern education, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo Galilei</a> was his tutor between 1605 and 1608. In 1608 Ferdinando arranged his marriage to Archduchess Maria Maddalena of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, daughter of Archduke Charles II. The wedding was marked by an elaborate display on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arno">Arno</a> that included a performance of the Argonautica, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a> sailed around an artificial island and gave Maria Maddalena six red apples, alluding to the Medici balls, or palle. The couple had eight children in eight years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ferdinando I died in 1609 and Cosimo succeeded him. Because of his precarious health he did not take an active part in governing and delegated administration to his ministers for most of his twelve-year reign, but he was a great patron of science and letters. He extended the Palazzo Pitti, rebuilt the Villa del Poggio Imperiale and enlarged the navy. The artist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-callot">Jacques Callot</a> worked at his court until 1621, recording feasts and carnivals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>.</p><p>In 1610 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a> was named court mathematician, a post that freed him from teaching mathematics at universities and let him work as philosopher, mathematician and astronomer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. He had dedicated his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidereus-nuncius">Sidereus Nuncius</a> to the grand duke, and the four moons of Jupiter he found were named the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilean-moons">Medicean Stars</a> in reference to Cosimo and his three brothers. Tuscan ambassadors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> received copies of the book and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telescope">telescopes</a> made by Galileo, paid for by the court treasury.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Cosimo died of tuberculosis on 28 February 1621. His elder son Ferdinando II was still a minor, and, as Cosimo wished, the regency went to Cosimo's wife and his mother. Of his children, Ferdinando II became grand duke, Margherita married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoardo-farnese-duke-of-parma">Odoardo Farnese, Duke of Parma</a>, and Anna married Ferdinand Charles, Archduke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>; two of his sons became cardinals.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cosimo II de' Medici” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Galileo named the four moons of Jupiter he discovered in 1610 the Medicean Stars, in honour of Grand Duke <b>Cosimo II</b> and his brothers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Galileo (1564–1642) taught at Pisa and Padua before becoming mathematician and philosopher to <b>Cosimo II de' Medici</b>, grand duke of Tuscany.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · People</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1610" aria-label="Florence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Cosimo II de' Medici"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5691 3800h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5691 3800h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1610">What was here in 1610? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
